# gtfs-compass ingest configuration.
# Copy to .env (gitignored) and fill in the required values. The ingest job
# reads these from its process environment — e.g. `set -a; source .env; set +a`
# or an equivalent in your cron wrapper.

# --- Required: Cloudflare D1 access -----------------------------------------

# Your Cloudflare account id (Cloudflare dashboard -> Workers & Pages ->
# right-hand sidebar, or `npx wrangler whoami`).
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=

# The D1 database id printed by `npx wrangler d1 create gtfs-compass`
# (also in api/wrangler.jsonc once configured).
CLOUDFLARE_D1_DATABASE_ID=

# An API token with the "Account -> D1 -> Edit" permission ONLY.
# Create at https://dash.cloudflare.com/profile/api-tokens -> Create Token
# -> Custom token. Scope it to this one account; no other permissions needed.
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=

# --- Optional ---------------------------------------------------------------

# Override the Mobility Database catalog CSV location.
# Default: https://files.mobilitydatabase.org/feeds_v2.csv
#MOBILITY_DB_CSV_URL=

# Comma-separated feed ids to static-ingest with `static` / `all`.
# Default: the curated seed list (currently "mta-subway,mta-bus,citibike").
#INGEST_STATIC_FEEDS=mta-subway,mta-bus,citibike

# Seconds between D1 API requests (stays under Cloudflare's global
# 1,200 requests / 5 minutes per-token limit). Default: 0.25
#D1_PACE_SECONDS=0.25

# Path of the local (same-host) run-lock file. Default: /tmp/gtfs-compass-ingest.lock
#INGEST_LOCK_FILE=/tmp/gtfs-compass-ingest.lock

# --- Worker / realtime layer (api/) -----------------------------------------
# The Worker reads feed config from D1 via its binding, and MTA subway
# realtime feeds require no API key. Deploy auth is handled by `wrangler
# login`. Worker vars below are set in api/wrangler.jsonc `vars` (or
# `wrangler secret put` where noted), not read from this file — listed here
# so self-hosters know they exist.
#
# The curated-feed allowlist is `vars.CURATED_FEEDS` in api/wrangler.jsonc
# (JSON array of feed ids). Only listed feeds are reachable through the
# public routes; adding an agency is a config change there plus a seed in
# ingest/src/gtfs_compass_ingest/seeds.py.

# Optional. Locate accuracy gate in meters: a resolved position coarser than
# this is returned as {"known": false}, never passed through. Also gates the
# /v1/departures walk heuristic: an origin fix coarser than this is ignored
# rather than used for a walk estimate. Default: 500
#LOCATE_MAX_ACCURACY_M=500

# Optional. Abort timeout in milliseconds for each locate provider fetch
# (BeaconDB has no SLA; a hung call must not eat the device's latency
# budget). Default: 2000
#LOCATE_TIMEOUT_MS=2000

# Optional. Shared secret gating the locate diagnostics surfaces
# (POST /v1/locate with log:true, POST /v1/locate/ref, GET /v1/locate/log).
# Set as a Worker secret — `cd api && npx wrangler secret put DIAG_TOKEN` —
# and send it as an `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header. Unset means the
# diagnostics surfaces are disabled. Never commit a real value.
#DIAG_TOKEN=

# Optional, NOT YET WIRED. Unwired Labs LocationAPI key for the locate
# chain's paid fallback slot (spec step 2). The provider slot is documented
# in api/src/locate.ts but deliberately unbuilt per the build order.
#UNWIREDLABS_KEY=

# Optional. Realtime API keys for feeds whose rows set rt_needs_key, as a
# JSON map of feed id -> key, set as a Worker secret:
#   cd api && npx wrangler secret put RT_FEED_KEYS
#   value: {"mta-bus": "<your MTA Bus Time developer key>"}
# MTA documents the Bus Time key as required (bustime.mta.info/wiki/Developers/GTFSRt)
# but does not currently enforce it (verified live 2026-08-03): without an
# entry the Worker polls keyless and logs one warning; a keyless 401/403
# logs a distinct enforcement-onset warning. Never commit a real key.
#RT_FEED_KEYS=

# --- Accounts and sessions (Phase 5) ----------------------------------------

# Optional, DANGEROUS. Single-user mode: the exact string "single" skips
# login entirely and binds every request to the fixed synthetic user
# `usr_single`. Anything unset, mis-cased, padded or unrecognized fails
# closed to normal multi-user auth.
#
# It is an auth bypass, not a convenience flag. The CSRF and Origin checks
# stay on, but those constrain BROWSERS only: with no credential in play,
# any non-browser client that can reach the Worker hostname is the account.
# It can read your named origins (home and office coordinates), post relay
# fixes to your devices, and delete the account. Workers deploy to a public
# hostname by default, so this is only safe behind a network-level control
# (Cloudflare Access, a private hostname, or equivalent).
# Set as a Worker var in api/wrangler.jsonc if you really want it — it is
# deliberately absent there so the default is multi-user.
#AUTH_MODE=single

# Optional. Sliding session window in days. A session is renewed only once
# it is past the half-life, so ordinary reads never write to D1. Default: 30
#SESSION_TTL_DAYS=30

# Optional. Absolute session lifetime in days, measured from when the
# session was first created. Rotation carries this anchor forward, so no
# amount of re-authentication walks a session past it. Default: 180
#SESSION_ABSOLUTE_TTL_DAYS=180

# --- Sign-in email delivery (Phase 5) ---------------------------------------
# Set these as Worker vars in api/wrangler.jsonc (or `wrangler secret put`
# where noted). REQUIRED for magic-link sign-in: with no provider selected,
# sign-in is disabled outright. There is deliberately no fallback — a
# provider that is named but unusable is a hard failure, never a silent
# downgrade to printing sign-in tokens into the log.
#
# NOTE: MailChannels' free Cloudflare Workers path was shut down in August
# 2024. Any tutorial showing a keyless POST to MailChannels is dead; there
# is no zero-config way to send mail from a Worker any more.

# Which delivery provider to use. Exact match on one of:
#
#   resend             HTTPS POST to Resend. Free tier is 100 emails/day.
#                      Needs RESEND_API_KEY and AUTH_EMAIL_FROM. Resend
#                      becomes a third-party processor of your users' email
#                      addresses — see the README's data section.
#   cloudflare-routing Cloudflare Email Routing's send_email binding. Zero
#                      cost, but it can ONLY deliver to addresses already
#                      verified as destinations in your zone's Email Routing
#                      settings — fine for a personal deployment, useless for
#                      open registration. Needs AUTH_EMAIL_FROM and an
#                      EMAIL_ROUTING send_email binding in api/wrangler.jsonc.
#   console            Local development only. Prints the sign-in link to the
#                      Worker log and echoes it in the response. Requires a
#                      non-empty AUTH_ALLOWED_EMAILS, so it can only ever
#                      print tokens for addresses you named.
#
# Anything unset, mis-cased or unrecognized fails closed: no mail is sent
# and no token is minted.
#AUTH_EMAIL_PROVIDER=resend

# Required by `resend` and `cloudflare-routing`: the envelope sender for
# sign-in mail. Must be an address on a domain you have verified with the
# provider, or delivery is rejected.
#AUTH_EMAIL_FROM=signin@example.com

# Required when AUTH_EMAIL_PROVIDER=resend. Create at
# https://resend.com/api-keys with "Sending access" only, then:
#   cd api && npx wrangler secret put RESEND_API_KEY
# Never commit a real key.
#RESEND_API_KEY=

# Optional. Registration allowlist: only these addresses can receive a
# sign-in link. Comma- or whitespace-separated, case-insensitive. An address
# that is not listed gets the exact same silent success response as one that
# is — sign-in never reveals who has an account. Leave it EMPTY to open
# registration to anyone (a deliberate choice, not the default).
# This is separate from console mode's allowlist requirement, which is
# unconditional: AUTH_EMAIL_PROVIDER=console refuses to start without a list.
#AUTH_ALLOWED_EMAILS=you@example.com,someone@example.com

# Optional. Sign-in emails per address per UTC day. A repeat request inside
# the token's 10-minute window resends the same unexpired token rather than
# spending another. Default: 5
#AUTH_SEND_BUDGET_ADDRESS=5

# Optional. The daily global send cap is split into two slices so that an
# attacker spraying unknown addresses cannot exhaust the provider's daily
# quota and lock existing users out of sign-in. KNOWN is reserved for
# addresses that already have an account; UNKNOWN covers everyone else.
# The defaults sum to 100/day, matching Resend's free tier — raise both
# together if your provider's quota is higher.
# Lockout is never terminal: the break-glass is for an operator to insert a
# `sessions` row directly (`npx wrangler d1 execute ...`) with a token hash
# they chose, then set the __Host-gc_session cookie to the token.
# Defaults: 80 and 20
#AUTH_SEND_BUDGET_KNOWN=80
#AUTH_SEND_BUDGET_UNKNOWN=20

# Optional. Origin the emailed sign-in link points at, e.g.
# https://compass.example — the link is built as
# <origin>/v1/auth/callback#<token>. Defaults to the origin of the request
# that asked for the link, which comes from the Host header; set this
# explicitly if the deployment answers on more than one hostname (a
# workers.dev name plus a custom domain, say) so every link points at the
# real front door. Set as a Worker var in api/wrangler.jsonc.
#AUTH_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://compass.example

# --- Firmware (firmware/) ---------------------------------------------------
# Firmware config does NOT read this file. WiFi credentials live in the
# device's NVS, provisioned over the serial console (`wifi_set`) or seeded
# from a gitignored firmware/sdkconfig.local:
#   CONFIG_GC_WIFI_SSID="your-network"
#   CONFIG_GC_WIFI_PASSWORD="your-password"
# See README "Firmware (Phase 4)" for the full flow.
